README

This sample demonstrates the PID control library in addon/pid. It does not need
any external hardware, because it controls a simulated process: a heated block
(first order plus dead time model) with a thermal time constant of 5 seconds, a
dead time of 0.5 seconds and some measurement noise. The control loop runs at
100 Hz in real time.

The sample runs through these steps:

1. Relay auto-tuning at 50 C, which determines the ultimate gain Ku and period
   Tu of the process. The PID gains are calculated for all supported tuning
   rules, the Tyreus-Luyben PID gains are used.
2. Holding the setpoint of 50 C.
3. Setpoint step to 70 C (the heater power saturates, anti-windup is active).
4. Disturbance: an additional heat loss (e.g. an open lid) is compensated.
5. Manual mode: the heater power is fixed to 30%, the temperature drops.
6. Bumpless transfer back to automatic mode, the setpoint is recovered.
7. Cool down to 40 C.

Every 0.5 seconds the setpoint (SP), the measured process value (PV), the
controller output (OUT, heater power in %) and the P, I and D terms are logged.
After each step the integral of the absolute error (IAE) and the maximum
overshoot are displayed. The whole demo takes about 2.5 minutes.

The addon/pid library has to be built first:

	cd addon/pid
	make

Configuration options (loop period, process model, tuning parameters and
tuning rule) can be modified at the top of kernel.cpp.

To control a real process, replace the class CHeaterPlant with reading your
sensor (e.g. an I2C temperature sensor) and writing your actuator (e.g. a PWM
output driving the heater). If the screen output delays the control loop too
much, log to the serial interface (logdev=ttyS1 in cmdline.txt) or reduce the
log interval.
